# Step 5: Anomaly Detector — Second Kafka Consumer

A standalone Python service that reads from the **same** `app-logs` Kafka topic (different consumer group) and flags error-rate spikes. This is the proof-of-value for Kafka's decoupling — two independent consumers, zero coordination.

## Architecture

```text
                                ┌─ Fluent Bit Consumer ─→ Loki ─→ Grafana
Pod stdout → Fluent Bit → Kafka ┤
                                └─ Anomaly Detector ─→ Postgres (anomaly_alerts)
```

The anomaly detector runs a **sliding 60-second window**. Every 10 seconds it evaluates whether the error rate (log lines with `levelname=ERROR` or events like `db_pool_exhausted`, `service_*_error`, `service_*_timeout`) exceeds a configurable threshold (default: 5 errors/window). When triggered, it writes an alert row to `anomaly_alerts` in Postgres and logs a structured JSON warning to stdout (which itself gets picked up by Fluent Bit → Kafka → Loki — visible in Grafana).

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The detector uses `confluent-kafka` (librdkafka-based) rather than `kafka-python` because `kafka-python` is unmaintained and has known issues with KRaft-mode brokers. This adds a new dependency to `requirements.txt`.

